<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altmark</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Altmark, 'old march', is a historical region in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stendal">Stendal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzwedel">Salzwedel</a> and Tangermünde. It was the remnant of the Northern March held by the margraves after the Slav rising of 983, and from it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> began the expansion east of the Elbe that created the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1157. It belonged to Brandenburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> until 1807.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altmark.jpg" alt="Altmark" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Björn Gäde at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schernikau_Altmark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
